  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nRain is ending from northwest to southeast this afternoon as a \nnorthern stream upper trough and surface low drag a cold front \nthrough the region. At the same time the northern stream upper \ntrough is merging into the southern stream trough responsible for \nthe rain, deepening and strengthening the over all system. Strong \nwinds have begun to develop in response to the tightening SPG and a \nstrong push of CAA. Widespread gusts of 30 to 40 mph are expected. \nThe strongest winds will be located over central South Dakota \nwhere gusts to 50 mph are possible. A Wind Advisory is in effect\nfor this area until 4 am Friday morning. The strongest winds \nwill move east overnight with the upper trough but should remain\nunder advisory criteria at 35 to 40 mph. Thanks to the mixy \nwinds and lingering clouds overnight lows tonight will remain in\nthe upper 30s to low 40s.\n\nEarly Friday morning there is a chance for additional light, \nscattered rain showers as moisture wraps around the surface low \nlocated over Minnesota. This combined with a second strong push of \nCAA will result in the LLJ increasing to 50+ kts over east central \nSouth Dakota and southwest Minnesota. An area of wind gusts up to 45 \nmph may be possible in southwest Minnesota. At this time, confidence \nin winds meeting advisory level criteria are too low to issue a \nheadline. But this will need to be monitored through Friday morning. \nWinds gradually taper off through Friday afternoon. Highs for Friday \nwill be below average for this time of year (average is low 60s) in \nthe upper 40s to low 50s. Clouds break up through the afternoon \nand skies will be clear overnight with nearly calm winds. \nRadiational cooling will allow lows to fall into the mid to low \n30s. Areas along and east of I-29 are likely to see temperatures\nfall to 30 degrees, therefore have issued a Freeze Watch from \nlate Friday night into Saturday morning. Precautions are \nrecommended for sensitive outdoor plants. Areas to the west of \nI-29 may have the potential for frost development, but \nconfidence was too low at this time to issue a Frost Advisory.\n\nAn upper ridge builds into the area for the weekend. Winds turn \nsoutherly and WAA will boost our highs back into the upper 50s to \nmid 60s. Lows will be in the 40s. Dry and warmer than average \nconditions continue Sunday through the middle part of next week. \nHighs will warm from the the mid 60s Sunday and Monday into the low \n70s Tuesday and Wednesday. Chances for rain return for the second \nhalf of next week.",
